add('''
### Finding 2 — the large discarded band and LD/block scale

[observation] Full released distribution (`docs/amy-binarized-results.md`): **356,335 variants**, **3.66%**, at 3.16<=|X|<=5.45 versus **31,939**, **0.33%**, above 5.45; **723** 250-kb blocks carry a strong variant, **9,285** carry band-only evidence. The earlier scored subset’s 356,329 is six fewer than the full-release band, not a disagreement about this finding. No exact |X|=5.45 variant is recorded, so < versus <= does not alter these counts. Band-only blocks are not 9,285 independent selected events.

[inference] **Review status: NOT RAISED for the band count, 3.16 cutoff, 250-kb block counts, or prioritization/recall cost.** Threshold calibration WAS RAISED repeatedly (V0 R1/R2/R3; V2 R1/R2); see §3b. LD overcounting WAS RAISED (V0 R1 p. 2, V2 R1 p. 8, V3 R1 p. 9), not as a missed-band survey. The closest general anticipation is the authors’ limited-power statement in §4 Finding 1. It would manufacture a connection to call the reviewer’s permissiveness objection a demand to lower the discovery threshold.

### Finding 3 — AMY carriage, power floor, binarization and GRM retention

[observation] The supplied ~94% carriage, within-history slope below the n=225 detection floor, X-equivalent ~3.64, and ~97% GRM retention are all supported with an important **cut-specific qualification** in `docs/amy-binarized-results.md` / `docs/local-ancestry-results.md`. Primary A<=1 gives carriage **0.946114**, within slope **0.0485754/kyr** versus floor **0.145764/kyr**, GRM-Z retention **96.955252%**, but its X-equivalent is **2.726085**, **not 3.64**. A<=2 sensitivity gives X-equivalent **3.637970**, carriage **0.942277**, within slope **0.0759689/kyr** versus floor **0.152380**, with essentially **100%**, not 97%, GRM retention. Both slopes are below their respective floors; only A<=2 crosses 3.16. Thus the task’s compressed numerical summary is true across analyses, but those values are not one fitted binary definition. Retention is a ratio of |Z|, not percentage of variance explained; our small-cohort GRM is not a faithful full-scan rerun.

[inference] **Review status: NOT RAISED or DISMISSED for AMY, n=225, binarization, detection floor, or the retention result.** General variance absorption/power was ANTICIPATED by authors (V0 response p. 23, quoted §3a), and source-only selection was treated as a negative control (V2 response p. 48). No direct response addresses the coexistence of high carriage and near-unattenuated binary signal. Our measurements therefore do not substantiate a universal GRM-absorption story; assay/tagging, sample size, selection timing and reporting threshold remain separate possibilities.

### Finding 4 — no overall low-|X| / high-replication excess

[observation] Project low-|X| replication pass **~4.3%** versus matched null **5%** (`docs/DECISIONS.md`, `docs/I-tier2-results.md`). This is a negative empirical result under our tested units/statistics/power. The precise Tier-1/Tier-2 rates **0.0427/0.0438** account for approximate 4.3%; neither differs materially from the supplied rounding.

[inference] **Review status: NOT RAISED, ANTICIPATED quantitatively, or DISMISSED.** The review never reports this quadrant/rate or tests independent ancient-history replication. Cross-ethnic/family polygenic replication (§3d) is a different question. Authors’ homogeneous-pressure natural-replicates explanation (V0 response p. 23, §3e) is related reasoning, not a prediction or measurement of our 4.3% result. Our negative result constrains our original over-correction hypothesis; author confidence in their scan cannot substitute for this independent project finding.

### Finding 5 — overlapping ancestry-collinear and carried-change blind spots

[observation] The earlier project capstone/decision asserts overlapping blind spots in the carried-change regime. `docs/amy-argument-reanalysis.md` later qualifies the stronger assertion of physical identity: selection within admixing populations can also align with ancestry, so overlap does not establish equality of all regimes. This evaluation preserves both entries instead of rewriting either.

[inference] **Review status: mechanistic ingredients RAISED/ANTICIPATED; specific overlap/equivalence NOT RAISED or SETTLED.** V0 R3 explicitly says the GLMM contains time-collinear directions (p. 5, §3a). Authors acknowledge variance absorption (response p. 23), do not detect source-only selection (V2 response p. 48), and acknowledge true ancestry-axis selection (V2 response p. 59). These support the relevance of the distinction, not the claim that ancestry-collinear selection and carried-in change are always the same physical situation. No referee/author compares the GLMM with our within-history statistic or proves their blindness regimes coincide.

### Finding 6 — Mies & Mathieson ancestry-deviation ambiguity

[observation] The supplied characterization of Mies & Mathieson (2026), also recorded in `docs/local-ancestral-state-method.md`, is that FADS1/SLC24A5 deviations support within-background change **or** underestimated ancestry shifts. That phrase is treated here as the task/project characterization, **not** a new verbatim quotation from their paper (which is outside the source being evaluated).

[inference] **Review status: specific Mies & Mathieson finding NOT RAISED.** `Mies` does not appear in extracted text; Mathieson appears in other earlier-work references. The general ambiguity WAS ANTICIPATED: V0 R2 raises mislocalized source-selection signals (p. 4) and asks for mixture-composition-versus-outlier decomposition (p. 5); V2 authors acknowledge true ancestry-associated selection and reject ancestry association as proof of incomplete correction (p. 59). None discusses Mies & Mathieson’s FADS1/SLC24A5 local ancestry estimates, local-ancestry measurement error or its specific two-way interpretation. Do not call this a review of that later result.

## 5. What the review did not settle

[inference] These are concrete open requests/weak responses, not a claim that publication required resolving every problem. The linked concern-table IDs and quotations delimit the gaps.
''')
